--TEST--
`Core\Session::start` with no `[session]` block opens the SQLite store in the data folder, and the session it opens reads back what it saved -- `rule:core-api/session-roster` and `rule:http-server/no-session-block-means-the-data-folder-store`
--GET--
under=a-request
--FILE--
<?nvs
// The case answers a request, because that is the first thing `start` checks
// (`rule:security/request-state-throws-in-an-isolate`).
//
// No `[session]` block is written, so the store is the data folder's
// `session.sqlite`. A bare `start()` opens a new, empty session there.
Core\Session::start();
echo "a new session has no value: ", Core\Session::get("visits") == null ? "yes" : "no", "\n";

Core\Session::set("visits", 1);
echo "the session reads back what it saved: ", Core\Session::get("visits"), "\n";

// A second `start()` on one request throws, as it does on every store.
uint $refused = 0;
try {
    Core\Session::start();
} catch (RuntimeError $twice) {
    $refused = $refused + 1;
}
echo "a second start was refused ", $refused, " of 1\n";
--EXPECT--
a new session has no value: yes
the session reads back what it saved: 1
a second start was refused 1 of 1
